# Simple and readable interface

## Clarity before density

- Clarity comes before density: a page must be understandable at a glance,
  without the user having to search for information.
- A page has ONE main objective and a single focus area. Secondary
  information is not stacked up: it goes behind a tab (`Tabs`) or into a
  detail sheet reached by a link.
- Limit the number of areas visible at the same time: a summary (a few key
  figures), then a single main list/table. The other sections move to
  secondary access.
- Natural reading order: header → summary → detail. Each area has a
  distinct role and a clear title.

## Zero overlap

- Areas must never overlap or superimpose on the screen.
- Reserve space with grid/flex and the Design System spacing tokens; never
  compress blocks to make everything fit.
- When space is missing, blocks wrap to the next line (clean vertical
  stacking) rather than shrinking until they touch.
- Check every intermediate screen width: no collision between cards,
  headers, filters and tables.

## For non-expert users

- A single main content column, at most one optional secondary column.
- Simple labels, readable figures, direct vocabulary (see
  `knowledge-base/ux/ui-copy.mdc`).
- Table limited to the columns useful for the task; the full detail goes
  to the detail sheet.
- Avoid accumulating cards, tables and filters side by side: this is the
  typical pattern of an unreadable page.

